alience prior (grounded in feature counts and familiarization base rates) remains common knowledge across all levels of recursion, allowing listeners to resolve complex implicatures while respecting prior salience.\", \"context\": \"round 1 candidate 3 refine chosen rsa_l2\"}\n\n(End of file - total 3 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"{\"name\": \"valence_salience_listener\", \"outcome\": \"admitted\", \"detail\": \"ELPD-LOO -21154.6\", \"hypothesis\": \"Listeners interpret the speaker's evaluative framing (favorite versus least favorite) as modulating expectations of referent complexity: people expect a speaker with positive valence to describe a feature-rich object, but expect a speaker with negative valence to describe a simple, feature-poor object. This valence-directed prior over referent richness guides both prior expectations and pragmatic inversion of the speaker's utterance.\", \"context\": \"round 1 candidate 1 explore\"}\n{\"name\": \"rsa_l2_shared_prior\", \"outcome\": \"admitted\", \"detail\": \"ELPD-LOO -21169.6\", \"hypothesis\": \"We refine depth-2 RSA (rsa_l2) by incorporating the shared salience prior from rsa_l1_shared_prior as common knowledge across all levels of recursive reasoning. Rather than assuming uniform object priors, speakers and listeners at every depth weight referents by their familiarization base rates and feature counts, allowing prior object expectations to guide pragmatic disambiguation as well as uninformative prior choices.\", \"context\": \"round 1 candidate 2 refine incumbent rsa_l2\"}\n{\"name\": \"rsa_l2_shared_prior_2\", \"outcome\": \"admitted\", \"detail\": \"ELPD-LOO -21117.1\", \"hypothesis\": \"We refine rsa_l1_shared_prior by extending its recursive pragmatic reasoning depth from depth 1 to depth 2, so that listeners invert a depth-2 speaker who simulates the depth-1 pragmatic listener rather than a literal listener. Under this mechanism, the salience prior (grounded in feature counts and familiarization base rates) remains common knowledge across all levels of recursion, allowing listeners to resolve complex implicatures while respecting prior salience.\", \"context\": \"round 1 candidate 3 refine chosen rsa_l2\"}","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/user/auto-psych/data/rsa/loop_smoke2/attempted_hypotheses.jsonl","text":"{\"name\": \"valence_salience_listener\", \"outcome\": \"admitted\", \"detail\": \"ELPD-LOO -21154.6\", \"hypothesis\": \"Listeners interpret the speaker's evaluative framing (favorite versus least favorite) as modulating expectations of referent complexity: people expect a speaker with positive valence to describe a feature-rich object, but expect a speaker with negative valence to describe a simple, feature-poor object. This valence-directed prior over referent richness guides both prior expectations and pragmatic inversion of the speaker's utterance.\", \"context\": \"round 1 candidate 1 explore\"}\n{\"name\": \"rsa_l2_shared_prior\", \"outcome\": \"admitted\", \"detail\": \"ELPD-LOO -21169.6\", \"hypothesis\": \"We refine depth-2 RSA (rsa_l2) by incorporating the shared salience prior from rsa_l1_shared_prior as common knowledge across all levels of recursive reasoning. Rather than assuming uniform object priors, speakers and listeners at every depth weight referents by their familiarization base rates and feature counts, allowing prior object expectations to guide pragmatic disambiguation as well as uninformative prior choices.\", \"context\": \"round 1 candidate 2 refine incumbent rsa_l2\"}\n{\"name\": \"rsa_l2_shared_prior_2\", \"outcome\": \"admitted\", \"detail\": \"ELPD-LOO -21117.1\", \"hypothesis\": \"We refine rsa_l1_shared_prior by extending its recursive pragmatic reasoning depth from depth 1 to depth 2, so that listeners invert a depth-2 speaker who simulates the depth-1 pragmatic listener rather than a literal listener. Under this mechanism, the salience prior (grounded in feature counts and familiar